.\" Conforms to POSIX.1-2017
.TH YYERROR 3 "2026-04-02" "IEEE/The Open Group" "POSIX Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
yyerror \- user-provided error handler for yacc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B void yyerror(const char *\fIs\fP);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR yyerror ()
function is a user-supplied routine called automatically by the
.BR yyparse (3)
function generated by
.BR yacc (1)
whenever a syntax error is localized during the parsing of an input stream.
.PP
When the parser transitions into an error state due to receiving an invalid sequential lookahead token from the lexical analyzer
.BR yylex () ,
it suspends normal parsing operations and delegates the immediate diagnostic responsibility to
.BR yyerror ().
The string parameter
.I s
contains a basic diagnostic message provided by the parser generator, commonly the generic string
.BR "syntax error" .
.PP
The user's implementation of this function is expected to display, log, or store the error message. It is common practice for developers to enhance the output by appending external state data, such as a source line number or filename context tracked exclusively by the lexical analyzer.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR yyerror ()
function does not return a value.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

extern int yylineno;
extern char *yytext;

void yyerror(const char *s) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Error at line %d near '%s': %s\\n",
            yylineno, yytext, s);
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yacc (1),
.BR yyparse (3),
.BR yynerrs (3)
